I recommend updating your Helm chart so your GKE deployment reproduces the routing behavior of your Nginx configuration:

- `/api/users/user` → User Service receives `/user`
- `/api/products/product` → Product Service receives `/product`
- `/api/orders/order` → Order Service receives `/order`
- Internal microservice calls continue using Kubernetes DNS, such as `http://product-service:80`.
- Each microservice uses `ClusterIP`; the external entry point handles incoming traffic.

The key change is to support URL prefix rewriting. Your current `ingress.yaml` routes requests but does not rewrite their paths. Because you're using GKE's Ingress API (`networking.k8s.io/v1`), I recommend using a GKE Gateway API `HTTPRoute` for native prefix rewriting, or keeping a separate NGINX Ingress Controller if you specifically want Nginx behavior.

I'll show the minimal changes to your existing Helm chart first, then explain the routing-controller choice.

## 1. Update `values.yaml`

Change your Ingress paths to the API prefixes and add Gateway configuration.

```
service:
  type: ClusterIP
  port: 80

ingress:
  enabled: true
  className: ""

  # Used by the HTTPRoute template below.
  gatewayName: ecommerce-gateway

  paths:
    - path: /api/users/
      service: user-service
      rewritePrefix: /

    - path: /api/products/
      service: product-service
      rewritePrefix: /

    - path: /api/orders/
      service: order-service
      rewritePrefix: /
```

Your existing `services`, image versions, resource requests, and health probes can remain unchanged.

These values define the desired routing, but the current Kubernetes Ingress template will not apply `rewritePrefix`. We'll replace that template with an `HTTPRoute` template.

## 2. Replace `templates/ingress.yaml`

Because you want the Nginx-style prefix stripping on GKE, use the Gateway API `HTTPRoute` resource.

```

{{- if .Values.ingress.enabled }}
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: ecommerce-route
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - name: {{ .Values.ingress.gatewayName }}
  rules:
    {{- range .Values.ingress.paths }}
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: PathPrefix
            value: {{ .path | quote }}
      filters:
        - type: URLRewrite
          urlRewrite:
            path:
              type: ReplacePrefixMatch
              replacePrefixMatch: {{ .rewritePrefix | quote }}
      backendRefs:
        - name: {{ .service }}
          port: {{ $.Values.service.port }}
    {{- end }}
{{- end }}

```

This template assumes that a compatible Gateway named `ecommerce-gateway` already exists in the same namespace. The Gateway must be configured with a supported GKE GatewayClass and an appropriate listener. Your current Ingress resource does not establish that prerequisite.

### Expected routing

Incoming request

`/api/products/product`

HTTPRoute rewrites the prefix

`/api/products/` → `/`

Backend request

`http://product-service:80/product`

This is the intended behavior of your original Nginx configuration. The same rule applies to users and orders.

## 3. Check your Service template

Your existing `templates/service.yaml` is already appropriate for internal service discovery:

```
spec:
  type: {{ $.Values.service.type }}
  selector:
    app: {{ $service.name }}
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: {{ $.Values.service.port }}
      targetPort: {{ $service.port }}
```

With `service.type: ClusterIP`, the routing is:

```
Gateway → product-service:80 → Product Pod:3000
```

Your environment variables can remain:

```
env:
  ORDER_SERVICE_URL: http://order-service:80
  USER_SERVICE_URL: http://user-service:80
  PRODUCT_SERVICE_URL: http://product-service:80
```

These internal URLs do not need the public `/api/...` prefixes.

## 4. Deploy and validate

First check whether Gateway API is available and whether you already have a Gateway:

```
kubectl get gatewayclass
kubectl get gateway -A
kubectl get httproute -A
```

If you don't have a Gateway, you must configure one with a supported GKE GatewayClass before applying the new route. Do not remove your existing Ingress until the new Gateway has a working address and the routes have been tested.

Then validate your chart:

```
helm lint ./helm/ecommerce1.1/

helm template microservices1.1 ./helm/ecommerce1.1/ \
  -n default
```

After you have configured the Gateway and reviewed the rendered resources, apply the changes:

```
helm upgrade microservices1.1 ./helm/ecommerce1.1/ \
  -n default

kubectl get httproute
kubectl describe httproute ecommerce-route
kubectl get gateway ecommerce-gateway
```

Test the actual endpoint after the Gateway reports that it is programmed and has an address:

```
curl -i http://YOUR_GATEWAY_IP/api/products/product
curl -i http://YOUR_GATEWAY_IP/api/users/user
curl -i http://YOUR_GATEWAY_IP/api/orders/order
```

Replace `YOUR_GATEWAY_IP` with the address reported by your Gateway.

My recommendation: if you want to keep your current GKE Ingress without introducing Gateway API, change the paths to `/api/users`, `/api/products`, and `/api/orders`, but update your Node.js services to accept those full paths. If the services must receive stripped paths exactly as in Nginx, use Gateway API rewriting or a dedicated NGINX Ingress Controller.